{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["The reason for <b>they saw not one another, neither rose any from his place</b> is that this darkness was not the absence of the light of the sun that shines upon them so that it was like night, but rather it was thick darkness — that is, a very dense cloud that descended from the heavens, for therefore Scripture says (Exodus 10:21), \"Stretch out your hand toward the heaven,\" to bring down from there a great darkness falling upon them. And it would extinguish every lamp, just as in all deep pits and in all places of intense darkness a lamp cannot endure, and likewise those who pass through mountains of darkness — no lamp and no fire at all stands for them there. Therefore <b>they saw not one another, neither rose any from his place</b>; for were it not so, they would have used lamps. And this is what Scripture says: \"He sent darkness, and it was dark\" (Psalms 105:28) — for it was a sending of darkness, not merely the fading of the light of day. And it is possible that it was a very dense cloud, palpable, that had a kind of substance to it, as the words of our Sages (Exodus Rabbah 14:1), just as it is in the Ocean, according to the testimony of R. Abraham."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "R\"E (ר\"א) resolved as R. Abraham [Ibn Ezra], who describes palpable darkness at sea in his own commentary on this verse. The word איד (usually 'calamity') is here understood as 'cloud' (עב), following a known medieval Hebrew usage; translated hyper-literally as 'cloud' to preserve the sense."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:05:35.690717813Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}